(CS 301: Distributed Systems - Lecture 8) Tj
T* (Raft Consensus and Replicated State Machines) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (1. The Core Consensus Challenge) Tj
T* (In a distributed architecture, independent servers must agree on values) Tj
T* (despite network partitions, packet loss, message delays, and server crashes.) Tj
T* (The fail-stop model assumes nodes fail cleanly rather than acting maliciously.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (2. Replicated State Machine Architecture) Tj
T* (Identical replica servers execute an identical sequence of deterministic) Tj
T* (commands from a shared replicated log. If each server starts in the same) Tj
T* (initial state, they will transition through identical intermediate states.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (3. Three Decomposed Subproblems in Raft) Tj
T* (Unlike Paxos, Raft explicitly decomposes consensus into three modules:) Tj
T* (A. Leader Election: Choosing one node to coordinate operations.) Tj
T* (B. Log Replication: The leader accepts writes and replicates them.) Tj
T* (C. Safety: Committed log entries must survive all future terms.) Tj
ET

