(CS 301: Lecture 8 \(Continued\)) Tj
T* (Server Roles, Quorum Math & Leader Completeness) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (4. Node State Machine & Logical Clock Terms) Tj
T* (Each node exists in one of three states: Leader, Follower, or Candidate.) Tj
T* (Time is divided into monotonically increasing terms acting as logical clocks.) Tj
T* (If a message arrives with a higher term, any node steps down immediately.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (5. Majority Quorum and Split-Brain Prevention) Tj
T* (A candidate must acquire strictly greater than N/2 votes to become leader.) Tj
T* (For example, in a 5-node cluster, quorum is at least 3 nodes.) Tj
T* (Because any two majorities must overlap by at least one node, it is) Tj
T* (mathematically impossible for two leaders to be elected in the same term.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (6. Randomized Election Timeouts \(150ms - 300ms\)) Tj
T* (Randomizing election timeouts breaks split-vote deadlocks effectively.) Tj
T* (Voters deny requests if the candidate's log is less up-to-date than their own.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (7. Common Exam Trap: Disk Write vs Commit) Tj
T* (A write to disk is NOT committed until replicated across a strict majority.) Tj
ET

endstream
